\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Consider estimating whether a content moderation policy reduces hate speech differently across demographic groups, using GPT-4 to classify thousands of posts.
After standard confusion-matrix correction, the analysis concludes the policy \emph{increases} hate speech among younger users---but the true effect is protective.
This sign reversal is not an edge case.

Large language models (LLMs) now routinely replace human annotators in computational social science (CSS), producing labels for sentiment, hate speech, stance, and political discourse at costs orders of magnitude below manual coding~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt,tornberg2024chatgpt,ziems2024nlp,tan2024llm}.
When these labels serve as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) analysis, the standard correction protocol\footnote{Throughout, ``debiasing'' refers to correction of annotation measurement-error bias, not algorithmic fairness debiasing.} is straightforward: validate a sample against expert annotations, estimate a confusion matrix, and apply its inverse uniformly to correct conditional average treatment effect (CATE) estimates.
Yet LLM annotations are not uniformly accurate: \citet{ashwin2025bias} document error rates varying by up to 5$\times$ across demographic subgroups; in our pilot, GPT-4.1 error rates range from 10.2\% to 20.0\% across text-feature subgroups ($\Delta\pi = 0.098$).

We show that this standard correction can make things worse.
In 14.9\% of our synthetic configurations (across a grid of $\Delta\pi$ and $\tau(0)$ values; $\tau(0) = 0.03$), global correction reverses the sign of estimated CATE---a protective treatment ($\tau = +0.03$) appears harmful ($\hat{\tau} = -0.059$)---rising to 31\% at $\tau(0) = 0.01$ (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}); all eight real LLMs tested amplify bias under global correction, while EC-HTE eliminates sign reversal entirely.
Global correction amplifies bias by up to 5.9$\times$ ($K{=}4$ with CausalForest); the classical Frazis-Loeb attenuation correction~\cite{frazis2003estimating} \emph{increases} bias by up to 2.9$\times$.
The mechanism is over-correction: pooled correction calibrated for the average error rate introduces substantial bias in low-error subgroups where the naive estimator is nearly unbiased.

We derive an exact bias identity explaining when and why (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}), verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$.
The per-subgroup correction prescribed by the identity reduces $|\text{bias}|$ by $7.4\times$ under extreme heterogeneity; the advantage is conditional on sufficient $\Delta\pi$ and expert budget.

Existing corrections---PPI~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi}, DSL~\cite{egami2024dsl}, PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci}, arm-specific PPI~\cite{mozer2026ppi}, ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv}---assume homogeneous prediction error; none addresses effect modifier misclassification (Section~\ref{sec:related}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sign_reversal.pdf}
\caption{CATE sign reversal under global correction ($K{=}2$, $\tau(0) = 0.03$). (A) Naive vs.\ global-corrected CATE; red triangles mark sign reversals (7.5\% of subgroup estimates). (B) Extreme case: true $\tau = +0.03$, estimated $\hat{\tau} = -0.059$. (C) 14.9\% of configurations exhibit sign reversal; all 8 real LLMs amplify bias; EC-HTE: 0\% reversal.}
\label{fig:sign_reversal}
\end{figure*}

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item An exact bias identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}): global correction introduces subgroup-level bias $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1-2\bar{\pi})$, explaining when and why it reverses CATE sign (14.9\% of configurations at $\tau(0) = 0.03$); verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$ across synthetic grids and 11 empirical LLM confusion matrices. The identity immediately yields a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) predicting when correction increases bias from error rates alone, without estimating treatment effects (Sections~\ref{sec:theory}--\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
    \item A ``when not to correct'' decision framework: per-subgroup correction outperforms global correction when $\Delta\pi > 0.15$, $n_{\text{expert}}/K \geq 50$, and $K \leq 4$; outside this envelope, the diagnostic guides practitioners to retain standard correction (Section~\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
    \item One concrete instantiation: hierarchical Bayesian confusion matrix estimation (EC-HTE), empirically verified for DIM, CausalForestDML, and R-learner, and composable with PPCI for joint covariate/outcome error (Sections~\ref{sec:method}--\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
\end{itemize}
Code and data generation scripts are publicly available.\footnote{\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ec-hte}}

\section{Correction Prescribed by the Identity}
\label{sec:method}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} identifies per-subgroup misclassification deviation as the bias source; the natural correction is per-subgroup confusion matrix inversion.
This correction (EC-HTE) proceeds in three stages: estimate per-subgroup confusion matrices via hierarchical Bayes, invert them to correct observed outcome moments, and propagate correction uncertainty into downstream causal estimators.
The complete procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:echte} (Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}) and illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:method_overview}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_method_overview.pdf}
\caption{EC-HTE pipeline. \textit{Top (red):} global correction applies a pooled confusion matrix uniformly, risking sign reversal. \textit{Bottom (blue-green):} EC-HTE estimates per-subgroup matrices via hierarchical Bayes, applies stratified inversion with a $\kappa$-guard, and propagates uncertainty into CausalForestDML.}
\label{fig:method_overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Hierarchical Bayes Confusion Matrix Estimation}

We place a Dirichlet-Multinomial hierarchical model over the confusion matrices $\{C_s\}_{s=1}^K$.
The model estimates the full $J \times J$ confusion matrix per subgroup and does not require symmetric error rates~(A2); (A2) simplifies only the diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}).
For each subgroup $s$, row $j$ of $C_s$ has a Dirichlet prior centered at the global pooled confusion matrix $\hat{C}_{\text{global}}$: $\text{Dir}(\alpha_0 \cdot \hat{C}_{\text{global},j})$, where $\hat{C}_{\text{global},j}$ is the $j$-th row of the pooled CM and $\alpha_0 > 0$ is a shared concentration parameter estimated via empirical Bayes (marginal likelihood maximization).
We place a $\mathrm{Gamma}(3, 1)$ hyperprior on $\alpha_0$, robust to $2\times$ perturbation (Appendix~\ref{app:hyperprior}).
Large $\alpha_0$ pulls estimates toward $\hat{C}_{\text{global}}$; small $\alpha_0$ allows subgroup-specific rates to dominate---partial pooling borrows strength while preserving heterogeneity.

Posterior inference uses empirical Bayes optimization ($<$5ms; MCMC alternative in Appendix~\ref{app:mcmc}).
HB estimation outperforms stratified MLE in 87.5\% of configurations (21 of 24) across the experimental grid (Section~\ref{sec:ec_hte_eval}), with the largest gains when $n_{\text{expert}} / K < 100$.
Below $n_{\text{expert}} \times P(Z_{\text{minority}}) / K < 25$, stratified MLE is preferred.

\subsection{Subgroup-Stratified Correction}

Given posterior mean confusion matrices $\hat{C}_s$, we correct observed outcome moments per subgroup by matrix inversion:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = \hat{C}_s^{-1} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s,
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = [\hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(0, s),\, \hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(1, s)]^\top$ collects the observed conditional outcome moments in subgroup $s$.
Invertibility requires $\pi_s \neq 0.5$ (A4); Laplace smoothing and a $\kappa$-guard ($\kappa(\hat{C}_s) > 100 \to$ fallback to $\bar{C}^{-1}$) ensure finite-sample stability.
All 13 tested LLM--dataset combinations fall in $\kappa \in (1.25, 3]$ with coverage $\geq$93\% (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_analysis}).

Global correction applies $\bar{C}^{-1}$ uniformly, introducing the $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau$ bias of Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}; subgroup-stratified correction eliminates this at the cost of higher variance (Appendix~\ref{sec:budget}).

\subsection{Variance-Adjusted CausalForest}

For heterogeneous treatment effect estimation, we feed corrected covariates into CausalForestDML~\cite{wager2018causal} with honest splitting.
Conditional on tree structure (determined by the splitting subsample), CausalForest CATE estimates are weighted averages of local moments; input bias corrections therefore propagate as weighted averages of per-subgroup corrections.
The adaptive bias from data-dependent splitting is asymptotically negligible~\citep{athey2019generalized}; our empirical $R^2 > 0.97$ across 520 configurations confirms this propagation in finite samples.
The forest produces per-observation standard error estimates $\text{se}_i$.
To account for confusion matrix correction, we propagate uncertainty through the inverse mapping:
\begin{equation}
\text{se}_{\text{corrected},i} = \sqrt{\text{diag}\!\left(\hat{C}_{S_i}^{-1}\, \text{diag}(\text{se}_i^2)\, \hat{C}_{S_i}^{-\top}\right)}.
\end{equation}
This plug-in SE conditions on $\hat{C}_s$, neglecting CM estimation uncertainty.\footnote{Independence between CausalForest and CM estimation error is approximate when expert labels overlap with training data; negligible at $n_{\text{expert}} / N < 0.1$ (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).}
Below $n_{\text{expert}}/K < 50$, a bootstrap ($B{=}100$) maintains $\geq$95\% coverage at all budgets tested (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic criterion.}
Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction harms subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from per-subgroup error rates alone (AUC~$= 0.86$; Section~\ref{sec:diagnostic}).

\paragraph{Composition with PPCI.}
EC-HTE corrects covariate misclassification; PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} corrects outcome-side error. Under both error types, composition reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.179 to 0.007, valid when error sources are independent (Appendix~\ref{sec:ppci}).
